    {
      "turn": 5,
      "doctor_question": "When you have these thoughts about the cameras and the neighbors, do you ever find that your own thoughts become jumbled or that you have trouble keeping your train of thought organized?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S021",
      "patient_response": "Yeah, I do. My thoughts get... well, they get really tangled up sometimes. Like, just the other day, my supervisor at work, he asked me a simple question. He just wanted to know which floors I had finished cleaning. And I tried to tell him, I really did, but then I started talking about the old bus route I used to take to get to the building. Route 42, the one that used to go past the community garden. \n\nI had a little plot there for a while, grew these amazing heirloom tomatoes. You can't buy tomatoes like that in the store, they taste like water. You gotta grow 'em yourself, get your hands in the dirt. My grandmother taught me that. She had this huge garden behind her house, right next to the church basement where they held the bingo nights. She'd win sometimes, too. She'd bring home these little plastic trinkets or maybe a five-dollar bill, and she was so proud of it. She always smelled like lavender and damp earth...\n\nBut, uh, where was I? Oh, right, my supervisor. Yeah, I was talking to him, and I could hear myself just going on about the church basement and the garden, and I realized I wasn't even answering his question about the floors. It's like my brain just takes a left turn and I can't steer it back.",
