    {
      "turn": 7,
      "doctor_question": "Aside from the times you feel quiet or flat, have you ever felt so low, sad, or hopeless that you felt like you couldn't get out of bed or stop crying for weeks at a time?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_2",
      "patient_response": "Yeah... yeah, I have. It's been like that a lot, actually. Ever since I moved into this new place, it's just been this heavy... this heavy feeling. Like I can't drag myself out from under it. For most of the time since the move, the sadness, the low energy... I just feel guilty about everything, and I don't care about the things I used to like. It's just been there in the background, you know? Even on the days when the voices in the stairwell or the worries about the hallway cameras are the main thing on my mind, that heavy sadness is still just sitting there. \n\nMoving is hard, though. I hired these movers, two young guys, and they broke my favorite lamp. It was this ugly brass thing, but my mom gave it to me when I got my first apartment. It had this fringed shade that gathered dust like crazy. I tried to fix it with superglue, but superglue never really holds brass together right, does it? You really need a proper weld for something like that. My dad could have fixed it. He had a welding torch in the garage, right next to where he kept the Mustang parts. He used to wear this big heavy welding mask, looked like Darth Vader or something. I went to see that movie in the theater when it came out, the first Star Wars. The line was wrapped all the way around the block. We stood out there for two hours in the heat, eating these stale pretzels from a street vendor... \n\nBut, uh, what was I saying? Right, the sadness. Yeah, it's just been there almost every day since I moved, just making it hard to get out of bed.",
